A||N||8||kornholio||testing ignition coil||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||12-09-1999||07:15 PM||||Is it possible to do the resistance tests (as outlined in the Haynes manual) with the coil still on the engine?  Or would that throw off the resistance numbers?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||RZURCTN||12-10-1999||08:38 AM||||I don't know for sure, but it is possible.  I would just follow the instructions in the Haynes manual and pull the coil  out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AL||12-10-1999||10:41 PM||||well if you want to test if coil is good,remove spark plug wire, if the spark is blue it's good if it looks yellow then it will eventually give out on you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kornholio||12-11-1999||09:40 AM||||Is it really neccessary to unplug the battery when removing the coil?  I just don't want to reset everything (alarm shock sensor, radio clock and presets).||||reg||5
Z||000004||fiero56||12-11-1999||07:39 PM||||I don't really see a need to disconnect the battery to do a lot of jobs.  I ususally don't either. If I were removing it on MY car, I would leave the battery connected.  But, I also don't want to be indirectly responsible for anything negative that may happen. I say leave the battery be, but others may have a different opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||09:40 PM||||Necessary?... No<P>Reccomended?... Yes<P>It is VERY easy to touch the pos. to the a metal component inadvertantly.  Usually you don't do any damage, but you do get a nice mark on your plenum or wrench... Just be careful.  I can just see the headlines now.. First the Fiero was a fiery-deathtrap, new discoveries have found it to be more like the electric chair!<P>LOL,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||12-11-1999||09:56 PM||||ESD precautions.  I fried my ignition module the first time I replaced my distributor tube o-ring, because I left the battery connected when I unplugged the coil from the distributor.  A 90 cent repair ended up costing me $70.  Might not happen every time, but why risk it?||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-11-1999||10:50 PM||||Before I had an alarm installed in my Fiero, I used to pull the coil/distributor line everytime I parked in a parking lot. I always left the battery connected, and it never shorted anything or even sparked.  Shouldn't the coil only be hot, when the ignition is in the start/run position?||||reg||5
Z||000008||2birds||12-12-1999||12:48 AM||||I don't think the coil to disributor spark wire will damage anything, but the ones from the ignition module to the coil are the ones I'm talking about.  I have no other way of explaining how an ignition module can fail so suddenly, while it is removed and re-installed in the car.  No outward physical damage.||||reg||1
